{"name":"Prairie Dog","hex":"#937067","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"prairie dog","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any member of the genus Cynomys, small, stout-bodied burrowing rodents with shallow cheek pouches, native to North America and Central America."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To pop up from a hole or analogous orifice in a manner that resembles the way a prairie dog pops its head up from its burrow.","To struggle to hold back an imminent bowel movement."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/prairie_dog"}],"entries":[{"title":"Prairie dog","blocks":[{"text":"Prairie dogs are herbivorous burrowing ground squirrels native to the grasslands of North America. There are five recognized species of prairie dog: black-tailed, white-tailed, Gunnison's, Utah, and Mexican prairie dogs. In Mexico, prairie dogs are found primarily in the northern states, which lie at the southern end of the Great Plains: northeastern Sonora, north and northeastern Chihuahua, northern Coahuila, northern Nuevo León, and northern Tamaulipas. In the United States, they range primarily to the west of the Mississippi River, though they have also been introduced in a few eastern locales. They are also found in the Canadian Prairies. Despite the name, they are not actually canines; prairie dogs, along with the marmots, chipmunks, and several other basal genera belong to the ground squirrels, part of the larger squirrel family (Sciuridae)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Black-Tailed_Prairie_Dog.jpg/330px-Black-Tailed_Prairie_Dog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prairie_dog"},{"title":"Prairie Dog (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Prairie Dog is the eighth album by American pianist and arranger Duke Pearson, and his second for the Atlantic label, recorded in 1966."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c9/Prairie_Dog_%28album%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prairie_Dog_(album)"},{"title":"Prairie","blocks":[{"text":"Prairies are ecosystems considered part of the temperate grasslands, savannas, and shrublands biome by ecologists, based on similar temperate climates, moderate rainfall, and a composition of grasses, herbs, and shrubs, rather than trees, as the dominant vegetation type. Temperate grassland regions include the Pampas of Argentina, Brazil, and Uruguay, and the steppe of Romania, Ukraine, Russia, and Kazakhstan. Lands typically referred to as \"prairie\" tend to be in North America. The term encompasses the lower and mid-latitude of the area referred to as the Interior Plains of Canada, the United States, and Mexico. It includes all of the Great Plains as well as the wetter, hillier land to the east. From west to east, generally the drier expanse of shortgrass prairie gives way to mixed grass prairie and ultimately the richer and wetter soils of the tallgrass prairie."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/James_Woodworth_Prairie.jpg/330px-James_Woodworth_Prairie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prairie"},{"title":"Dog","blocks":[{"text":"The dog is a domesticated descendant of wolves. Also called the domestic dog, it was selectively bred during the Late Pleistocene by hunter-gatherers. Dogs and the modern gray wolf share a common ancestor. Dogs were the first species to be domesticated over 14,000 years ago, before the development of agriculture, though genetic studies suggest the domestication process may have begun over 25,000 years ago. Due to their long association with humans, dogs have gained the ability to thrive on a starch-rich diet that would be inadequate for other canids."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Huskiesatrest.jpg/330px-Huskiesatrest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dog"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Cynomys_distribution.png/500px-Cynomys_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Cynomys_distribution.png/500px-Cynomys_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACynomys_distribution.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Prairie_Dog_Washington_DC_1.jpg/500px-Prairie_Dog_Washington_DC_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Prairie_Dog_Washington_DC_1.jpg/1280px-Prairie_Dog_Washington_DC_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Prairie_Dog_Washington_DC_1.jpg/500px-Prairie_Dog_Washington_DC_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Prairie dogs raise their heads from their burrows in response to disturbances.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APrairie_Dog_Washington_DC_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Cynomys_gunnisoni_Attilly.jpg/500px-Cynomys_gunnisoni_Attilly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Cynomys_gunnisoni_Attilly.jpg/1280px-Cynomys_gunnisoni_Attilly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Cynomys_gunnisoni_Attilly.jpg/500px-Cynomys_gunnisoni_Attilly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbn